# First off, comments seem to work, as in the program skips comments
# This will print only a 5. 
Print 5.

# This will lead to an error, that being, the line ended early
Print 5

# This will also lead to an error, that being it cannot detect a valid statement, because print != Print
print 5.

# This is also invalid because there is not at least one space between the Print and 5, so while adding letters, it found a number
Print5.

# Ironically this is valid, because there's at least one space between Print and 5. 
Print        5. 

# Again, not a "valid statement word"
Printt 5.

# This one adds 1 + 2 together!
Print 1 + 2. 

# This one subtracts 2 - 1!
Print 2 - 1.

# The same logic for spacing exists with addition and subtraction, as does the other cases like incorrect statement words, periods, etc. 

# Also we can do multi-long digit numbers. And multiple addition and subtraction
Print 10 + 100 - 20 - 10 + 10.

# The code properly sees the second plus sign. While tokenizer doesn't because it's seperated, here, the parser finds the logical error
Print 1 + + 2.

# Same thing with subtraction, and also, again, while the tokenizer is only built for making tokens, parser is able to figure it out
# even with multiple duplicate operators
Print 1 - - - 2. 

# Just as a reminder, you need a valid statement at the beginning or else it doesn't run
1 + 2.

# Since division isn't supported at the moment (neither is subtraction), this line leads to an error
Print 10 + 2 / 2.

# Expected number after plus sign, but found none, so error, so it catches this well. 
Print 5 + 

# No space between ++, would've entered a expect number error anyways
Print 5 ++ 5.

# Like with Print. which ends too early or ++ which currently would read as an error, +. will also send an error because it expects a space
Print 5 + 5 +.

# This has now been fixed. Lack of operator leads to error!
Print 5 5.

# This now has been handled, since nothing follows print it will lead to error. 
Print # 5. 

